The time period of oscillations of magnet

T=2 \pi \sqrt{\left(\frac{I}{M H}\right)}

where $I=$ moment of inertia of magnet $=\frac{mL^{2}}{12}(m$, being the mass of magnet $)M=$ pole strength $\times L$ and $H=$ horizontal component of earth's magnetic field. When the three equal parts of magnet are placed on one another with their like poles together, then

\begin{aligned}
I^{\prime} &=\frac{1}{12}\left(\frac{m}{3}\right) \times\left(\frac{L}{3}\right)^{2} \times 3 \\
&=\frac{1}{12} \frac{mL^{2}}{9}=\frac{I}{9}
\end{aligned}

and $\quad M^{\prime}=$ pole strength $\times \frac{L}{3} \times 3=M$
Hence, $T^{\prime}=2 \pi \sqrt{\left(\frac{I / 9}{M H}\right)}$
\begin{aligned}
\Rightarrow \quad T^{\prime} &=\frac{1}{3} \times T \\
T^{\prime} &=\frac{2}{3} \mathrm{~s}
